---
import Work from '../../layouts/Work.astro';
import BrowserFrame from '../../components/BrowserFrame.astro';
import FlowDiagram from '../../components/FlowDiagram.astro';
---

<Work
  title="AIRA"
  category="AI Product · Automation"
  summary="An AI career platform for students, combining job discovery, matching, application workflows and personalized career assistance in one place."
  description="AIRA — an AI career platform for students combining job discovery, matching, application workflows and personalized career assistance."
  year="2025 — present"
  role="Founder — product, positioning, growth and content systems"
  status="Live product, in active development"
  stack={['Next.js', 'Vercel', 'LLM APIs', 'HH integration', 'WordPress (content)']}
  ogImage="/images/aira-app.png"
  schemaType="SoftwareApplication"
  links={[
    { label: 'Visit AIRA', href: 'https://student-aira.com' },
    { label: 'Open the app', href: 'https://my-student-aira.com' },
  ]}
>
  <BrowserFrame
    src="/images/aira-app.png"
    alt="AIRA product interface: headline, early-access badge and the registration panel"
    url="my-student-aira.com"
    loading="eager"
    ratio="16 / 10"
  />

  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    Job search is a process problem before it is a matching problem. A student
    applying to their first role repeats the same sequence dozens of times: find a
    listing, decide whether it is worth applying to, tailor an application, send it,
    and then try to remember where everything stands. The tooling that exists is
    mostly built for experienced professionals who already know what they are looking
    for.
  </p>
  <p>
    AIRA started from that gap — the group with the least leverage in hiring is also
    the group with the least time and the least guidance.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Students lose momentum in the repetitive middle of the process, not at the start.
    Discovery, filtering and application tracking are spread across job boards,
    documents and browser tabs, and none of it carries context forward. Nothing
    remembers what you already applied to, what you decided about a role, or what to
    do next.
  </p>

  <h2>What I built</h2>
  <p>
    A career platform that keeps the whole search in one place: job discovery through
    an HH integration, saved roles, application workflows and personalized assistance
    on top. Rather than a job board with an AI feature bolted on, the intent is that
    the assistant sits inside the workflow the student is already running.
  </p>

  <FlowDiagram
    caption="Product flow"
    steps={[
      { label: 'Discovery', note: 'Roles pulled in via HH' },
      { label: 'Matching', note: 'Relevance to the student' },
      { label: 'Application', note: 'Workflow per role' },
      { label: 'Dashboard', note: 'State and next steps' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    This is my own product. I work on the product definition, positioning, interface
    direction and the growth side — including the content system that runs the
    public site, which is powered by the{' '}
    <a href="/work/ai-wordpress-publisher">content pipeline</a> I built for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    AIRA is live and in active development. Students can run their search from a
    single dashboard instead of a spreadsheet and a dozen browser tabs, and the
    surrounding content operation runs on automation rather than manual publishing.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'm working on next</h2>
  <p>
    Deepening the assistance layer so it does more of the repetitive judgement work —
    and broadening discovery beyond a single source.
  </p>
</Work>
